<h2>What Section 24 actually says</h2>
<p>The provision is one sentence: Sections 15 to 23 shall have effect notwithstanding anything inconsistent therewith contained in any other law for the time being in force. Read with the non-obstante language inside Section 16 itself — which overrides agreements as well as laws — the delayed-payment code operates as mandatory law. Parties cannot contract out of it, and other statutes yield to it within its field.</p>

<h2>The protected code, section by section</h2>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Provision</th><th>What it guarantees</th><th>What it defeats</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Section 15</td><td>Payment by the agreed date, capped at 45 days from acceptance or deemed acceptance</td><td>Credit-period clauses longer than 45 days</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Section 16</td><td>Compound interest, monthly rests, three times the bank rate, from the appointed day</td><td>Interest-waiver and lower-rate clauses</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Section 17</td><td>The buyer's liability to pay the amount with interest as above</td><td>Netting and set-off devices designed to erase statutory interest</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Section 18</td><td>Reference to the Facilitation Council: conciliation, then arbitration</td><td>Exclusive jurisdiction clauses and rival contractual arbitration mechanisms</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Section 19</td><td>75% pre-deposit for any application setting aside a Council award or decree</td><td>Attempts to litigate the award without securing the supplier</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Sections 22–23</td><td>Disclosure of unpaid MSME dues in buyers' accounts; interest not deductible as expenditure</td><td>Silent balance sheets and tax absorption of default interest</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>How the courts have applied the override</h2>
<p>The Supreme Court's decision in <em>Gujarat State Civil Supplies Corporation v. Mahakali Foods</em> settled the central collision: where the MSMED Act applies, its Section 18 mechanism prevails over an independent arbitration agreement between the parties, and a Council acting under the Act proceeds notwithstanding the contractual clause. The same logic runs through the pre-deposit jurisprudence under Section 19 — the 75% deposit is a condition that cannot be bargained away — and through the consistent refusal of courts to enforce interest-waiver clauses against the Section 16 entitlement. The override is, however, directional: it protects the statutory floor for the supplier; it does not create claims the Act itself does not confer.</p>

<h2>Where buyers still have room</h2>
<div class="check">
<ul>
  <li><strong>Status:</strong> whether the claimant held the requisite enterprise status for the supplies in question remains a live threshold issue, with the case law on registration timing still developing.</li>
  <li><strong>Merits:</strong> quality disputes, short supply, debit notes and performance failures are fully arguable — Section 24 protects the payment code, not the claim's merits.</li>
  <li><strong>Scope:</strong> transactions outside "goods supplied or services rendered" to the buyer — pure trading questions, works outside the Act's definitions — invite genuine contest.</li>
  <li><strong>Procedure:</strong> the Council's own compliance with the conciliation-then-arbitration sequence under Section 18 is reviewable, though ordinarily before the tribunal or in a Section 34 challenge rather than by writ.</li>
</ul>
</div>

<div class="note">
<p><strong>Practice note:</strong> Suppliers should plead Section 24 together with the specific non-obstante language of Section 16 whenever a buyer invokes contract clauses — the two provisions together answer the credit-period, interest and forum defences at the threshold. Buyers, for their part, should concentrate objections where the override does not reach: supplier status, the genuineness of the dues, and documented quality disputes raised contemporaneously rather than after the Samadhaan reference arrives.</p>
</div>
